# Stripe Payment Integration Rules

## SDK & Configuration

- Use the official `stripe` npm package — never raw HTTP calls
- Pin the API version explicitly: `apiVersion: '2026-03-25.dahlia'` (or latest stable)
- Store `STRIPE_SECRET_KEY` and `STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET` in environment variables only
- Use `sk_test_` keys during development; never commit live keys
- Initialize Stripe once in a shared module (`lib/stripe.ts`), export the instance

```typescript
// lib/stripe.ts
import Stripe from 'stripe';

export const stripe = new Stripe(process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY!, {
  apiVersion: '2026-03-25.dahlia',
  typescript: true,
});
```

## Edge Runtime / Cloudflare Workers Compatibility

When deploying to edge runtimes (Cloudflare Workers, Vercel Edge, Deno):

- MUST use `Stripe.createFetchHttpClient()` — the default `NodeHttpClient` hangs in V8 isolates
- MUST use `constructEventAsync` with `Stripe.createSubtleCryptoProvider()` for webhook verification
- The synchronous `constructEvent` will throw `CryptoProviderOnlySupportsAsyncError` in edge runtimes

```typescript
// lib/stripe.edge.ts — for edge/worker environments
import Stripe from 'stripe';

export const stripe = new Stripe(process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY!, {
  apiVersion: '2026-03-25.dahlia',
  httpClient: Stripe.createFetchHttpClient(),
});

export const webhookCryptoProvider = Stripe.createSubtleCryptoProvider();
```

## Checkout Sessions

### Subscriptions (recurring)
- Set `mode: 'subscription'` when at least one line item has a recurring price
- Pass all Price IDs in the `line_items` array (max 20 recurring + 20 one-time)
- One-time items in subscription mode are charged on the initial invoice only
- Always set `success_url` and `cancel_url` with `{CHECKOUT_SESSION_ID}` template variable
- Use `customer` param if user already has a Stripe customer ID; use `customer_email` for new users

### One-time Payments
- Set `mode: 'payment'` for single purchases
- Create `PaymentIntent` server-side, never client-side

### Mixed Cart (subscription + one-time add-ons)
- Use `mode: 'subscription'` and include both recurring and one-time price IDs in `line_items`

```typescript
// Example: create checkout session
const session = await stripe.checkout.sessions.create({
  mode: 'subscription',
  customer: existingCustomerId ?? undefined,
  customer_email: existingCustomerId ? undefined : userEmail,
  line_items: [
    { price: priceId, quantity: 1 },
  ],
  success_url: `${baseUrl}/billing/success?session_id={CHECKOUT_SESSION_ID}`,
  cancel_url: `${baseUrl}/pricing`,
  subscription_data: {
    metadata: { userId },
  },
  allow_promotion_codes: true,
});
```

## Price Configuration

### Tier Structure
- Define Products in Stripe Dashboard or via API, one per logical plan
- Create Prices for each billing interval: `month`, `year`, and optionally a one-time `lifetime` price
- Use `lookup_key` on prices for stable code references (e.g., `pro_monthly`, `pro_annual`)
- Annual pricing: offer 16–20% discount vs monthly to increase LTV
- Lifetime: use `mode: 'payment'` with a one-time price, not a subscription

### Example Tier Map
```typescript
// config/pricing.ts
export const PLANS = {
  free: { stripePriceId: null, limits: { projects: 3, storage: 100 } },
  pro: {
    monthly: { stripePriceId: 'price_xxx', amount: 1900 },
    annual:  { stripePriceId: 'price_yyy', amount: 15900 },
  },
  business: {
    monthly: { stripePriceId: 'price_aaa', amount: 4900 },
    annual:  { stripePriceId: 'price_bbb', amount: 41900 },
  },
} as const;
```

## Free Tier Management

- Do NOT create a Stripe Customer or Subscription for free-tier users
- Store plan status in your database: `plan: 'free'` flag on the user/org record
- Enforce free-tier limits in your application logic, not via Stripe
- Stripe only enters the picture when a user upgrades to a paid plan
- On upgrade: create Stripe Customer → create Checkout Session → sync via webhooks

### Entitlement Pattern
```typescript
// lib/entitlements.ts
type Plan = 'free' | 'pro' | 'business';

const ENTITLEMENTS: Record<Plan, Record<string, number | boolean>> = {
  free:     { maxProjects: 3, maxStorage: 100, analytics: false },
  pro:      { maxProjects: 50, maxStorage: 5000, analytics: true },
  business: { maxProjects: -1, maxStorage: -1, analytics: true },
};

export function canAccess(plan: Plan, feature: string): boolean {
  return !!ENTITLEMENTS[plan]?.[feature];
}

export function getLimit(plan: Plan, feature: string): number {
  return (ENTITLEMENTS[plan]?.[feature] as number) ?? 0;
}
```

## Customer Portal

- Use the Stripe-hosted Customer Portal for subscription management — saves weeks of UI work
- Configure in Dashboard: enable "Switch plan", "Update quantities", "Cancel subscription"
- Enable proration for plan switches; configure downgrades to apply at end of billing period
- Generate portal sessions server-side:

```typescript
const portalSession = await stripe.billingPortal.sessions.create({
  customer: stripeCustomerId,
  return_url: `${baseUrl}/billing`,
});
// Redirect user to portalSession.url
```

## Upgrade / Downgrade Flows

- NEVER cancel-and-recreate subscriptions to change plans — use `stripe.subscriptions.update()`
- For upgrades: use `proration_behavior: 'always_invoice'` to charge immediately
- For downgrades: use `proration_behavior: 'create_prorations'` or schedule at period end
- Always preview proration before executing: `stripe.invoices.createPreview()`
- When switching monthly ↔ annual: decide on `billing_cycle_anchor: 'now'` vs `'unchanged'`

```typescript
async function changePlan(subscriptionId: string, newPriceId: string, isUpgrade: boolean) {
  const subscription = await stripe.subscriptions.retrieve(subscriptionId);
  const currentItemId = subscription.items.data[0].id;

  return stripe.subscriptions.update(subscriptionId, {
    items: [{ id: currentItemId, price: newPriceId }],
    proration_behavior: isUpgrade ? 'always_invoice' : 'create_prorations',
  });
}
```

## Webhook Handling

### Security
- ALWAYS verify webhook signatures — no exceptions, even in development
- Endpoint path: `/api/stripe/webhooks` (or `/api/webhooks/stripe`)
- Read the raw request body (not parsed JSON) for signature verification
- Return `200` immediately; process business logic asynchronously for long operations

### Idempotency
- Store processed `event.id` values in a `stripe_events` table with a unique constraint
- Check for duplicates before processing — Stripe may deliver the same event multiple times
- Use Redis with 24h TTL for high-throughput idempotency checks (covers Stripe's 12h retry window)

### Edge Runtime Webhook Handler
```typescript
// app/api/stripe/webhooks/route.ts
import { stripe, webhookCryptoProvider } from '@/lib/stripe.edge';

export const runtime = 'edge';

export async function POST(request: Request) {
  const body = await request.text();
  const signature = request.headers.get('stripe-signature')!;

  const event = await stripe.webhooks.constructEventAsync(
    body,
    signature,
    process.env.STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET!,
    undefined,
    webhookCryptoProvider,
  );

  // Idempotency check
  const existing = await db.stripeEvent.findUnique({ where: { eventId: event.id } });
  if (existing) return new Response('Already processed', { status: 200 });
  await db.stripeEvent.create({ data: { eventId: event.id, type: event.type } });

  switch (event.type) {
    case 'checkout.session.completed':
      await handleCheckoutComplete(event.data.object);
      break;
    case 'customer.subscription.updated':
      await handleSubscriptionUpdate(event.data.object);
      break;
    case 'customer.subscription.deleted':
      await handleSubscriptionCanceled(event.data.object);
      break;
    case 'invoice.payment_failed':
      await handlePaymentFailed(event.data.object);
      break;
  }

  return new Response('OK', { status: 200 });
}
```

### Node.js Runtime Webhook Handler
```typescript
// For standard Node.js runtime (not edge)
import { stripe } from '@/lib/stripe';

export async function POST(request: Request) {
  const body = await request.text();
  const signature = request.headers.get('stripe-signature')!;

  const event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(
    body,
    signature,
    process.env.STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET!,
  );
  // ... same event handling as above
}
```

### Essential Webhook Events
| Event | Action |
|---|---|
| `checkout.session.completed` | Provision access, store customer/subscription IDs |
| `customer.subscription.created` | Sync plan to database |
| `customer.subscription.updated` | Handle plan changes, status transitions |
| `customer.subscription.deleted` | Revoke paid access, revert to free tier |
| `invoice.payment_failed` | Notify user, start dunning flow |
| `invoice.paid` | Confirm payment, extend access |
| `customer.subscription.trial_will_end` | Send trial-ending notification (3 days before) |

## Metered / Usage-Based Billing

### For New Integrations (2026)
- Stripe recommends **Metronome** for all new usage-based billing implementations
- Metronome handles: event ingestion, metering, rating, invoice generation, prepaid credits, enterprise contracts

### For Existing Billing Meters
- Send meter events with a deterministic `identifier` for idempotency
- Stripe aggregates usage server-side — do not aggregate yourself
- Track usage in a separate ledger table in your database for auditability

```typescript
// Report usage event to Stripe Billing Meters
await stripe.billing.meterEvents.create({
  event_name: 'api_calls',
  payload: {
    stripe_customer_id: customerId,
    value: '1',
  },
  identifier: `${customerId}_${requestId}`, // idempotency
});
```

## Data Architecture

- Store `stripeCustomerId` and `stripeSubscriptionId` on your user/org record
- Keep billing and subscription tables separate — they serve different query patterns
- Never store card numbers, CVVs, or full payment details anywhere in your system
- Subscription status should be a Zod discriminated union, not a simple enum
- Your database is the source of truth for entitlements; webhooks keep it in sync

## Validation & Type Safety

- Validate all webhook payloads with Zod schemas
- Use TypeScript strict mode with Stripe's built-in types
- Never trust client-side subscription status or price calculations — always verify server-side
- Validate `price_id` values against your known set before creating checkout sessions

## Anti-Patterns — NEVER Do These

- Do NOT build custom card input forms — use Stripe Checkout or Payment Elements
- Do NOT store card numbers or CVVs anywhere in your system
- Do NOT skip webhook signature verification, even in development
- Do NOT rely on client-side subscription status — always verify server-side
- Do NOT cancel and recreate subscriptions for plan changes — use subscription update
- Do NOT make direct Stripe API calls from React Server Components — centralize in `lib/stripe`
- Do NOT create Stripe customers for free-tier users
- Do NOT expose `STRIPE_SECRET_KEY` or raw webhook payloads to the client
- Do NOT use synchronous `constructEvent` in edge runtimes

## Testing

- Always test with Stripe test mode keys (`sk_test_`, `pk_test_`)
- Use Stripe CLI (`stripe listen --forward-to`) for local webhook testing
- Use Stripe test clocks for simulating billing cycle progression
- Test these flows: signup → checkout → webhook → access provisioned
- Test payment failure → dunning → recovery and cancellation → free tier revert
- Use Stripe's test card numbers: `4242424242424242` (success), `4000000000000002` (decline)
